RULING OWED — x-second-deploy-manifest. Two deploy manifests claim one target tree:

  ~/.claude/.agent-forge/deploy-manifest.json   2026-08-04, retired brand
  ~/.claude/.forge/deploy-manifest.json         2026-08-05, live

40 of 40 targets in the retired-brand record are also claimed by the live one — total
shadowing, not overlap. The pruner removes only what it can account for having written, so
every path the stale record owns is unattributable and will never be pruned.

WHY THIS IS NOT MINE TO TAKE: deleting a deploy manifest is a host-side, irreversible act
outside the workspace, and which record is authoritative carries a value dimension — the
stale one may hold the only account of artifacts the live one never wrote.

THE FORK, with my pick: the live `.forge/` record is authoritative — newer, brand-current,
and a superset (43 entries to 40). Confirm the 3 entries unique to `.forge/`, then delete
`~/.claude/.agent-forge/` entirely; the 40 shadowed paths are byte-identical claims, so
nothing is lost. Say the word and I will run it.

CONSEQUENCE WHILE THIS STANDS: `x-second-deploy-manifest` is a FILED stub — written with
¬census ∧ ¬re-slice, so it has no edge in R, no `promote` can reach it, and `done(P)` is
unreachable while it stands. That is praxis's frontier law third disjunct (`f06c9283`), and
it is why this plan sits at frontier = ∅ without being finished. The plan is at a TERMINUS,
